WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:05.120
In the summer of 1910, the western United
States ignited.

00:00:05.925 --> 00:00:12.200
A perfect blend of hot, dry weather, and hurricane
force winds fed the blazes - eventually resulting

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.950
in a terrifying firestorm.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:20.430
In just two days, 3 million acres burned,
full towns were turned to ash, and nearly

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.370
90 people died.

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:28.140
Its devastation was so dramatic that it shaped
US forest policy for years to come.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.394
Especially for one new agency.

00:00:32.394 --> 00:00:38.190
After the 1910 blaze, the US Forest Service
primarily focused on stopping fires.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.210
And at first, they were really effective.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:46.820
For decades, most accidental and natural forest
fires were stopped as quickly as they began.

00:00:47.776 --> 00:00:50.089
But today, we’re facing this:

00:00:50.089 --> 00:00:53.467
"Thousands of firefighters still battling
dozens of fires in the west."

00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:55.720
"More than 85 thousand acres burned."

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.890
"The worst wildfire season in state history."

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:03.600
Scientists are beginning to understand that
decades of fire suppression has created dense

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.989
forests that are ready to ignite 
in catastrophic ways..

00:01:07.989 --> 00:01:11.966
One proposed solution dates back hundreds
of years:

00:01:11.966 --> 00:01:14.553
burning parts of our forests,
on purpose.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.608
"So remember!

00:01:17.608 --> 00:01:21.131
Only you can prevent forest fires."

00:01:21.131 --> 00:01:26.401
In 1944, the US Forest Service introduced
Smokey the Bear - the goal was to educate

00:01:26.401 --> 00:01:29.939
the public on the dangers of forest fires.

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:34.030
But even before that famous campaign, the
federal government had put thousands of men

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:37.869
to work building fire towers and suppressing
burns.

00:01:37.869 --> 00:01:43.030
In 1935, the Forest Service even established
the “10 AM policy” - a rule that states

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:47.856
all fires should be suppressed by 10am the
day after they’re reported.

00:01:47.856 --> 00:01:52.770
This mentality that “all fire is bad”
drove an entire generation’s understanding

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.109
of what fire means to a forest:

00:01:55.109 --> 00:01:59.200
“A tragedy that happens year after year
in our great American forest areas."

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.259
But by the 1960s, scientists realized that
narrative isn’t entirely accurate.

00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:10.950
KOLDEN: For most of the globe and particularly
the forests in the western US, they actually

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.936
evolved with fire.

00:02:12.936 --> 00:02:17.930
Tree rings, soil samples, and charcoal records
all show a history of fire - likely started

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.970
by lightning strikes and other natural phenomena.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:26.420
And that history shows that fire isn’t always
a destructive force - but an important part

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:27.823
of forest ecology.

00:02:27.823 --> 00:02:35.930
KOLDEN: Fire plays a really important role
where it is essentially removing a lot of

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:43.560
the older, less productive material from the
landscape and is creating pockets in patches

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.520
of different ages of vegetation.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:54.460
KOLDEN: That diversity of ages also produces a
diversity of species. 

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.280
In many places, fires work as a “reset button”
for the forest.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:03.370
It clears out dead brush and older materials
and makes space for new growth.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:07.250
For some species, fire is even vital for reproduction.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:13.835
For example, low burning fires dry out sequoia
tree cones enough for them to open and drop seeds.

00:03:13.835 --> 00:03:18.840
At the same time, the fire clears the ground
to expose fresh, nutrient rich soil which

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.690
creates ideal conditions for seeds to germinate.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:28.380
But aside from the ecological benefits, fires
also help a forest become more resistant to

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:31.530
high severity burns later on.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:35.845
Low burning fires clear the forest floor 
and lower branches from trees. 

00:03:35.845 --> 00:03:39.268
Then, if a more intense fire moves through
the same area, 

00:03:39.268 --> 00:03:41.221
it’s slowed by a lack of fuel.

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:43.579
KOLDEN: So when you get those type of patches
all over the place,

00:03:43.579 --> 00:03:49.063
it becomes difficult for fire to move through 
that landscape again any time soon.

00:03:51.342 --> 00:03:55.870
But decades of suppression have led to
a build up of dry, dead materials in our forests

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:00.257
- so we’re long past that point of just
letting all fires burn.

00:04:00.257 --> 00:04:04.959
KOLDEN: We've got a forest that has not seen
fire, but that it evolved with fire.

00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:08.390
KOLDEN: And so there's all of this build up in the
forest of extra fuel.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:14.052
KOLDEN:  And it is climate change exacerbating those
extreme conditions.

00:04:14.052 --> 00:04:17.721
There’s just no simple reset button for
a century of suppression...

00:04:17.721 --> 00:04:20.180
or a changing climate.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:26.204
So in order to better adapt to a hotter, drier
future, some are turning to a centuries old practice.

00:04:26.655 --> 00:04:32.189
Indigenous people have long understood the
value of fire, and, for millennia before colonization,

00:04:32.189 --> 00:04:34.361
would burn sections of forest on purpose.

00:04:34.361 --> 00:04:39.219
AZZUZ: We have a history of our men burning
our hunting grounds.

00:04:39.219 --> 00:04:43.300
Women will burn to restore their food gathering
sites.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:51.138
So, you know, burning is just a key tool that
we use as indigenous people to keep our environment healthy.

00:04:51.138 --> 00:04:56.659
But colonists began placing strict laws against
burning as early as 1850 - criminalizing the

00:04:56.659 --> 00:04:58.681
long-held practice.

00:04:58.681 --> 00:05:03.860
After the 1910 blaze, legislation against
all burning only became more strict.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:07.425
AZZUZ: Indigenous people could be shot for
burning.

00:05:07.425 --> 00:05:11.879
And now, we’re looking to them
to lead the way.

00:05:11.879 --> 00:05:15.958
Prescribed or controlled burns draw on those
cultural indigenous practices.

00:05:15.958 --> 00:05:20.271
AZUZZ: Most people think, oh, these guys just
go out and burn whenever they want to.

00:05:20.271 --> 00:05:22.458
God, I wish it was that easy

00:05:22.458 --> 00:05:26.080
AZUZZ: We go before the counties to get their
permits.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.431
AZUZZ: We go before our tribal councils to get their
permission and their permits to burn on tribal land.

00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:34.139
AZUZZ: We go before the tribal air quality.

00:05:34.139 --> 00:05:36.358
AZUZZ: We go before the county's air quality.

00:05:36.358 --> 00:05:41.580
AZUZZ: And then I work with NOAA to predict my weather
throughout that week while we're burning.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.210
AZUZZ: Once we have ignition, everybody falls
in the place they need to be in.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:47.470
AZUZZ: They have their tools ready,

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:49.270
they have their radios on,

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:52.270
we all know where everybody is at every moment.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:56.990
The fire started in these controlled burns
moves slowly across the forest floor and clears

00:05:56.990 --> 00:05:59.360
out the buildup of dead material.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.629
If the wind shifts, or fire gets too high
it’s quickly contained and put out by the

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:05.059
trained crew.

00:06:05.059 --> 00:06:08.249
There's not one piece of our day that isn't
choreographed.

00:06:08.249 --> 00:06:15.541
In 2020 and 2021 lawmakers introduced bills to increase the use of controlled burning nationwide.

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:19.219
It’s a start, but it’s won't be enough on it’s
own.

00:06:19.219 --> 00:06:26.300
Recent data shows wildfires are only getting
larger and more destructive year after year.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:30.824
Campaigns like Smokey the Bear weren’t wrong
to teach us to be careful with fire.

00:06:30.824 --> 00:06:36.487
Today, research shows that human carelessness
like loose cigarettes, campfires, and sometimes

00:06:36.487 --> 00:06:41.259
even arson cause over 85% of disastrous wildfires.

00:06:41.259 --> 00:06:45.050
More controlled burns could help minimize
those risks.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:49.411
But we also need to recognize that fire is
a natural part of the landscape we’re now

00:06:49.411 --> 00:06:54.419
living in - and climate change is only going
to make it worse.

00:06:54.419 --> 00:06:58.449
That means building resilient communities
and passing legislation that requires fire

00:06:58.449 --> 00:07:05.000
resistant materials in high-risk areas and
truly educating the public on fire safety.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:09.559
AZUZZ: I..Yeah, saw Smokey Bear as a child
and kind of thought that that was going to

00:07:09.559 --> 00:07:11.169
go in the wrong direction.

00:07:11.169 --> 00:07:16.400
But here I am as an adult, realizing that
Smokey really frightened an entire population

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.189
of people into realizing, if we do this, something's
going to happen to us.

00:07:20.189 --> 00:07:21.259
AZUZZL: We're going to lose our homes.

00:07:21.259 --> 00:07:23.780
AZUZZ: We're going to destroy our environment.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:29.146
AZUZZ: Well, by not doing it, we're losing our homes
and our environment is being destroyed.

00:07:34.511 --> 00:07:38.601
The Forest Service has a program called the
Roots and Shoots program where

00:07:38.601 --> 00:07:41.953
they burn their grass for their basket weavers.

00:07:41.953 --> 00:07:45.000
Their grass is what gives us the white in our baskets

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.829
and it's a serotinous plant  — if it's not burned,
it's not useable.

00:07:48.829 --> 00:07:52.123
Our basket gathering sites are
on a rotation,

00:07:52.123 --> 00:07:55.674
so every three to five years those are burned

00:07:55.674 --> 00:08:01.715
because for the first three years you can continue
to gather hazel sticks in a certain site where it's been burned. 

00:08:01.715 --> 00:08:05.001
Other plants don't require fire that often. 

